Yes, this is another Reactor-based retry, so I would handle it the same way as the other WebClient classes.

Here, the retry logic is currently inside TrackingResourceWebClientService:

.retryWhen(createExponentialRetry(3, Duration.ofSeconds(2)))

and these methods are also local:

createExponentialRetry()
isRetryable()

Recommended approach

Since this application has a dedicated config package, create:

service
 └── config
      └── RetryConfig.java

Then move the common retry logic there.

RetryConfig

package com.cardinalhealth.advship.tracking.usps.edge.service.config;

import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClientResponseException;
import reactor.util.retry.Retry;
import reactor.util.retry.Retry.RetrySignal;

import java.time.Duration;

@Slf4j
@Configuration
public class RetryConfig {

    public Retry createRetry(int maxAttempts, Duration initialDelay) {
        return Retry.backoff(maxAttempts, initialDelay)
                .maxBackoff(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                .jitter(0.5)
                .filter(this::isRetryable)
                .doBeforeRetry(this::logRetry)
                .onRetryExhaustedThrow((spec, signal) -> {
                    logRetryExhausted(signal);

                    return new RuntimeException(
                            "Retries exhausted for the request",
                            signal.failure());
                });
    }

    public boolean isRetryable(Throwable throwable) {

        if (throwable instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
            int statusCode = ex.getStatusCode().value();

            return statusCode >= 500 && statusCode < 600;
        }

        return true;
    }

    private void logRetry(RetrySignal signal) {

        Throwable failure = signal.failure();

        log.error(
                "Retrying Tracking Resource API request. " +
                "retryAttempt={}, exceptionType={}, message={}, statusCode={}",
                signal.totalRetries() + 1,
                failure.getClass().getSimpleName(),
                failure.getMessage(),
                getStatusCode(failure),
                failure);
    }

    private void logRetryExhausted(RetrySignal signal) {

        Throwable failure = signal.failure();

        log.error(
                "Retries exhausted for Tracking Resource API request. " +
                "totalRetries={}, exceptionType={}, message={}, statusCode={}",
                signal.totalRetries(),
                failure.getClass().getSimpleName(),
                failure.getMessage(),
                getStatusCode(failure),
                failure);
    }

    private Integer getStatusCode(Throwable throwable) {

        if (throwable instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
            return ex.getStatusCode().value();
        }

        return null;
    }
}

Then modify your WebClient service

Add:

private final RetryConfig retryConfig;

Your constructor with @RequiredArgsConstructor will automatically inject it.

Then change:

.retryWhen(createExponentialRetry(3, Duration.ofSeconds(2)))

to:

.retryWhen(
    retryConfig.createRetry(
        3,
        Duration.ofSeconds(2)
    )
)

So:

return webClient.post()
        .uri(getURI(endpoint, aspectName))
        .header(HttpHeaders.CONTENT_TYPE, MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
        .header(HttpHeaders.ACCEPT, MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
        .bodyValue(jsonBody)
        .retrieve()
        .bodyToMono(WebhookRecordResponse.class)
        .retryWhen(
            retryConfig.createRetry(
                3,
                Duration.ofSeconds(2)
            )
        )
        .onErrorMap(this::handleErrorMap)
        .block();

Then remove these from the WebClient service

You no longer need:

private Retry createExponentialRetry(...)

or:

public boolean isRetryable(...)

That leaves the service focused on making the API call and the config responsible for retry behavior/logging.

Resulting flow

TrackingResourceWebClientService
              ↓
       retryWhen()
              ↓
          RetryConfig
              ↓
       isRetryable()
              ↓
       Retryable?
        ↙       ↘
      YES        NO
       ↓          ↓
doBeforeRetry    Error
       ↓
log exception
+ retry counter
+ status code
       ↓
     Retry
       ↓
  failure again
       ↓
     log again
       ↓
Retries exhausted
       ↓
final exception logged

One thing to note: this class's existing handleErrorMap() already logs the final error. So after implementing logRetryExhausted(), you may see the final exception logged twice—once by RetryConfig and once by handleErrorMap(). I'd run a local test first; if they're duplicates, keep the retry-attempt logs in RetryConfig and decide whether the exhaustion log needs to remain there or can rely on the existing handleErrorMap() logging.